when we think of military interventions  in the cold war it's often those of the  superpowers  such as the usas in vietnam or the ussrs  in afghanistan  yet one nation which maintained a  long-standing military presence in  another country was cuba  whose troops stayed and fought in angola  for over a quarter of a century  but why so the first question is why  angola  well as many of you will know it used to  be part of the portuguese empire as of  the late 20th century that empire wasn't  doing all too well and the people of  angola were fighting to get rid of the  portuguese  the two main groups doing this were the  mpla who were communist and junita who  were not  in 1975 the portuguese announced that  they were going to leave angola forever  which meant it was civil war time prior  to independence the cuban government had  supported the mpla by providing some aid  in the form of military attaches and  doctors  it didn't send any overt military  assistance since that could risk war  with portugal but when they had left in  the civil war had begun the cubans had a  much freer hand  their reason for such involvement was  ideological cuba wanted there to be more  communist states in the world and unlike  the ussr's leadership fidel castro  believed that africa was particularly  well positioned for communism  another question some might be asking is  why specifically angola are not  somewhere closer to home  it was a literal ocean away and there  were other much closer fights involving  communists that cuba could help with  and the reason they didn't was because  direct involvement in south or central  america would provoke the ire of the us  and was best left to the soviets whereas  the fight in angola was far enough away  that the us was less  interested in direct involvement that's  not to say that the u.s wasn't  interested in angola it was  but matters were complicated when the  war spilled over to neighbouring  countries and eventually invited the  attention of south africa  south africa which was trying to  suppress an independence movement in  namibia sent troops into angola in late  1975 to stop aid coming from the north  and also because they didn't like  communists  the mpla didn't fare too well against  south africa's troops and so castro  decided to directly intervene  and at this point the angolan civil war  largely became a proxy war between south  africa and cuba  south african involvement did make  intervention by the us much more  difficult since apartheid was not  popular internationally and so the us  directly aiding the country enforcing it  was not a good look  after a couple of years angola proper  was mostly secured and south africa had  retreated  the civil war expanded again and in 1983  south african troops returned to aid  unita  and cuba responded to this by sending  tens of thousands of its own soldiers to  angola  years of continuous fighting culminated  in the 1987 battle of quito cuaneva  which was the decisive mpla cuban  victory  and after this angola south africa and  cuba decided to negotiate because this  war was getting expensive and also  south africa was losing here it was  decided that the mpla would be the ones  to run angola south africa would leave  and cuba would withdraw its military  forces  direct foreign involvement in the  angolan civil war was now mostly over  and cuba had achieved the long-term  changes in angola that it wanted  for about a year when the mpla dropped  its commitment to communism but still  a good effort i hope you enjoyed this  episode and thank you for watching with  a special thanks to my patrons  james bissonette sam kelly moneymaker  the pastry section danny maloney moe  marvin cassal rob waterhouse james  castaneda jordan longley spinning three  plates gustav swan jerry lamdin rasheed  ali marcus asner izzy maggie paxkowski  cooling castleman david silverman copper  tone anthony beckett matthew shipley  lexi schwinn robert wetzel skye  chappelle winston 